// ── THE SPARKS BECOME SUPPLY, WHICH IS WHAT PUTS SERVING ON THE FABRIC ────────────────────────
//
// product.supplier.ubicloud is the precedent and this is the same work for a supplier we own:
// normalize what a serving group actually offers into the fabric's carrier, so selection has
// something real to rank. Until a Spark group is a SupplierOffer, every question about where a turn
// runs has to be answered somewhere else -- which is how this repository ended up with two placement
// authorities that disagreed.
//
// IT RESTATES NOTHING ABOUT THE GROUP. The served model, the head, the units and the engine are
// gunbc.spark.pair_serving_desired's facts and stay there; this module consumes them.
//
// AN EARLIER ATTEMPT WENT THE OTHER WAY and the shape of the mistake is reusable: it minted a
// serving-specific placement outcome, candidate and admissibility verdict, all of which already
// existed in product.fabric.selection as SupplySelection, CandidateOffer and OfferCandidacy, and the
// re-minted admissibility was strictly weaker -- a string reason where the fabric carries typed arms.
// Whether a group serves the requested model needs no new type at all; it is capability fungibility,
// and supply.dag already answers it with CapabilitiesNotOffered.

// ── THE OFFER'S IDENTITY IS THE LAUNCH, NOT THE GROUP ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
// This is the correction that mattered most. An offer keyed on the group wire makes group A's
// invocation 1 and group A's invocation 2 THE SAME OFFER SUBJECT, despite differing in KV state,
// queue state, runtime provenance and effective batch policy. Every observation this module puts on
// an offer -- readiness, its timestamp, its receipt -- then survives a restart it should not have
// survived, and a previous launch's evidence prices or authorizes its successor. A restart must
// create a new offer subject, and keying on the launch is what makes that structural rather than a
// rule someone remembers.
fn spark_serving_offer_key(launch: VllmEndpointProcessLaunch) -> NonEmptyStr {
  vllm_endpoint_process_launch_key(launch: launch)
}

// ── WHAT WE SANCTION THE GROUP FOR ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// work.dag is explicit that the capability reference on the OFFER side is our own fleet knowledge
// and never a supplier claim. For a box we deploy ourselves that is straightforwardly true.
//
// THE CAPABILITY IS THE TOKEN-SERVING OPERATION, NOT THE MODEL. An earlier spelling derived it from
// a model name, and because the demand side had to carry an equal reference for fungibility to
// match, the harness stamped the offer with whatever the CURRENT DEMAND required: one launch became
// three SupplierOffers -- any-admitted, exact-DeepSeek, exact-GLM -- under one offer key, its
// capability chosen by the buyer. An offer's facts are the supplier's. The model the launch serves
// is carried beside the offer as observed evidence (SparkOfferProjection.served_model), the caller's
// model requirement is request identity (gunbc.serving.turn_demand ServingTurnRequest), and the
// screen between them runs before any offer is bound.
fn spark_serving_capability() -> CapabilityManifestRef {
  "gunbc.capabilities.spark-serving.token-serving" as CapabilityManifestRef
}

// A PROJECTION BESIDE THE OFFER, NOT A SECOND KIND OF OFFER -- ubicloud's naming caution, applied.
// These are named for the Spark rather than generically because two supplier bindings is not yet
// proof of a shared concept, and a generic minted now would be a bare alias.
type SparkOfferProjection<P> {
  offer: SupplierOffer<P>
  launch: VllmEndpointProcessLaunch
  served_model: NonEmptyStr
}

// ── AN OWNED GROUP'S QUOTE IS ZERO, AND THAT IS A FACT RATHER THAN A PLACEHOLDER ──────────────
//
// The Sparks are owned outright, so the cash that must be RESERVED before a grant issues is nothing:
// no meter starts when a turn lands. selection.dag draws exactly this line -- liability is the axes
// that actually move money, while the comparison number additionally carries opportunity cost and
// valued delay.
//
// So two admitted groups tie at zero and the ordering falls to the tie policy, which is the correct
// answer for serving under this cut: the thing that distinguishes group A from group B is whether
// either is occupied, and that question is settled at admission before any offer is priced.
//
// AND THE DEMAND SIDE HAS TO AGREE OR NOTHING CLEARS: a reservation price below every offer refuses
// them all, so a serving demand's buy terms are zero against this zero. Zero against zero is the
// honest pair rather than two numbers chosen to meet -- recorded here, beside the quote it has to
// match, because the demand constructor that would have carried it consumed nothing and is gone.
fn spark_serving_quote() -> OfferQuote {
  QuotedPerSecond(MoneyRate { amount: money_amount_micro(count: 0), currency: Usd })
}

data spark_serving_billing_quantum: Second = second(count: 1)

// ── READINESS IS OBSERVED OR THE OFFER DOES NOT EXIST ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
// An offer is a claim that work placed on this executor will run, and a group whose engine has not
// been observed serving is not making that claim. Binding one anyway would put a candidate in front
// of selection that nothing established -- the fabricated placement the whole exercise exists to
// remove, arriving one layer earlier.
type SparkServingReadiness
  = ServingObserved { ready_at: Timestamp, receipt: ObservationReceiptRef }
  | ServingUnobserved { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

// READINESS IS A PROJECTION OF AVAILABILITY, NEVER A SECOND READING, AND ONLY FOR THE PROCESS IT WAS
// READ FROM. gunbc.serving.serving_availability is the one statement of whether a route can take new
// work; an offer on a launch is ready exactly when that reading is available AND names that same
// endpoint process. An available reading from another process refuses naming both, so an answer from
// process A can never make an offer on process B ready.
fn spark_serving_readiness_of(verdict: ServingFrontDoorAvailabilityVerdict, launch: VllmEndpointProcessLaunch) -> SparkServingReadiness {
  let availability = verdict.reading
  match availability {
    ServingAvailable { endpoint: _, launch: observed, observed_at: t, receipt: r } =>
      if vllm_endpoint_process_launch_is(launch: observed, other: launch) {
        ServingObserved { ready_at: t, receipt: r }
      } else {
        ServingUnobserved { obligation: join([
          "availability was observed from endpoint process ", vllm_endpoint_process_launch_key(launch: observed) as String,
          " but the offer names ", vllm_endpoint_process_launch_key(launch: launch) as String,
        ], "") as NonEmptyStr }
      }
    ServingUnavailable { endpoint: _, observed_at: _, cause: _, receipt: _ } =>
      ServingUnobserved { obligation: serving_availability_text(a: availability) as NonEmptyStr }
    ServingAvailabilityUnread { endpoint: _, attempted_at: _, cause: _ } =>
      ServingUnobserved { obligation: serving_availability_text(a: availability) as NonEmptyStr }
  }
}

// ── THE ROUTE IS OBSERVED OR THE OFFER DOES NOT EXIST, WHICH IS READINESS'S ARGUMENT AGAIN ────
//
// SupplierOffer carries a ProviderRoute because admission is decided over provider x service x
// region and never over a company. A route's region is therefore an OBSERVATION, exactly as
// product.fabric.provider_route provider_route_from_region treats it: unread, it refuses as
// ProviderRouteRegionUnobserved rather than filling the slot. This module has the same obligation
// and, today, no observer -- so it refuses for the same reason the readiness arm above refuses.
// Binding an offer whose route nothing established would put a candidate in front of selection
// carrying a region no one measured, and every admission decision downstream would be taken
// against it.
//
// THE NEARBY NAME THAT IS NOT THIS ONE: gunbc.spark.serving_deployment_selection
// serving_topology_region is a DecisionRegion from the Pareto vocabulary -- a region of a decision
// field, not a place a machine sits. Reusing it here would be one name for two concepts.
// THE THIRD ARM IS WHERE THE SPARKS ACTUALLY SIT, AND IT IS A SEPARATE ARM PRECISELY SO THAT
// NOBODY HAS TO TRUST AN ANNOTATION. A unit the operator owns and physically holds has no possible
// observer of its site: there is no vendor to ask and no API to read, because the site is what the
// operator says it is. The honest carrier for that is an ATTRIBUTABLE ALLOCATION -- decider, date,
// basis -- which is what gunbc.spark.dgx_procurement OperatorAllocation already is, and which
// gunbc.spark.first_party_serving fills for every Spark unit from the operator's 2026-09-11
// assertion. Putting it in the observed arm would have relabelled an allocation as a measurement,
// and the divergence (DESIGN section 3b) would then have been readable only in prose; as its own
// arm, every consumer that cares can tell the two apart by matching, and the offer records which
// kind of fact its region rests on.
// A FOURTH ARM, AND IT EXISTS BECAUSE THE TWO HALVES OF A ROUTE ARE ESTABLISHED BY DIFFERENT
// EVIDENCE. The region comes from the operator's dated site allocation, which is about WHERE THE
// MACHINES ARE and is established independently of whatever answered on them. The service is a claim
// about WHICH DEPLOYMENT ANSWERED, and nothing about host coverage establishes it. Until this arm
// existed the standing had no way to say the first without also asserting the second, so an
// unreadable readback on covered hosts received the pair-serving service by default -- the same
// declaration-standing-in-for-a-realization defect this module was changed to remove, surviving in
// the half nobody had looked at.
// WHICH ROUTE-BEARING ARM IS REACHABLE, AND FROM WHAT. ServingRouteObserved is unreachable from the
// standing: no producer observes the region of a unit the operator holds. ServingRouteOperatorAsserted
// needs an EndpointIncarnationObservation, and gunbc.spark.serving_incarnation_observe now constructs
// one in production over the fleet key session (it was previously reachable only from fixture
// evidence). The production caller, gunbc.harness.harness_seat harness_serving_realization, takes the
// standing that producer returns: on a host where the observation is obtained the asserted arm is
// reachable, and on one where it is not -- no fleet context, the inspect grant not held, hidepid, or
// no process matching the exported start -- the producer returns EndpointIncarnationUnobserved with
// that specific cause and this standing refuses. The arm carries the observation it rests on, so an
// asserted route can always be traced to the incarnation that answered.
type SparkServingRoute
  = ServingRouteObserved { route: ProviderRoute }
  | ServingRouteOperatorAsserted { route: ProviderRoute, allocation: OperatorAllocation, answered_by: EndpointIncarnationObservation }
  | ServingRouteServiceUnidentified {
      allocation: OperatorAllocation,
      cause: SparkServiceIdentityCause,
      obligation: NonEmptyStr,
    }
  | ServingRouteRegionUnobserved { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

// THE SINGLE PLACE THE STANDING IS RECORDED, which is why the 2026-09-11 restoration replaced one
// declaration rather than every call site -- the same property that made the withdrawal cheap.
//
// THE OBLIGATION ROW SURVIVES THE RESTORATION, AND WHAT IT IS FOR NOW IS NARROWER THAN IT WAS. No
// production producer returns it any more -- the standing below refuses with the uncovered-host or
// unreadable-membership obligation instead, which say WHICH group is not covered rather than that
// nobody observes regions at all. Its one consumer is the binder fixture in
// test.claim.spark.spark_serving_offer_route_witness_test, where it is the spelling of "this route's
// region rests on nothing" that drives the binder's refusing arm. That is a real consumer and the
// row stays; calling it "the refusal a caller reaches" would claim a live role it no longer has
// (review 64014).
data spark_serving_route_region_obligation: NonEmptyStr = "gunbc.spark.serving_offer: no producer observes which region a serving host sits in, so the offer's ProviderRoute cannot be constructed and the offer does not bind"

// WITHDRAWN 2026-09-09, RESTORED 2026-09-11 ON AN ASSERTION RATHER THAN AN OBSERVATION. The
// withdrawal was right: what it refused was a region nobody had established. What changed is not
// that somebody measured one -- for self-hosted units nobody ever can -- but that the operator
// stated where the units are, dated and attributable, and gunbc.spark.first_party_serving carries
// that statement as an OperatorAllocation over the Spark units it names.
//
// THE STANDING IS PER GROUP, NOT GLOBAL, BECAUSE THE ASSERTION IS PER UNIT. It takes the hosts the
// offer would run on and refuses unless the allocation covers every one of them. The first spelling
// took no argument at all, which made an eight-unit roster decorative and would have let a group on
// a machine the operator never placed at his home carry a region citing an assertion about other
// machines (review 63995).
//
// THE SUBJECT IS THE REALIZATION THAT ANSWERED, NOT A HOST LIST, because the same hosts can carry
// more than one deployment and the route's facts are about the one being offered. Passing a bare
// host list let the region and the service come from different deployments (operator-routed review
// of #11083).
//
// WHAT EACH INPUT DECIDES. The hosts decide the REGION, against the operator's dated allocation. The
// SERVICE needs the incarnation observation, which ties the answering endpoint to one engine launch.
// Given that observation, the declared unit digest decides whether that launch came from our unit,
// and the readback decides whether its configuration agrees. Without the observation, the readback
// only shapes the refusal's diagnosis. No container environment is attributed to anything here.
//
// AN EMPTY HOST LIST REFUSES, and that arm is the reason this is a fold over the UNCOVERED set
// rather than an all() over the covered one: all() of nothing is true, so a group whose membership
// could not be read would have passed the check vacuously -- the check reading cleanest exactly
// where it knows least.
data spark_serving_route_hosts_unreadable_obligation: NonEmptyStr = "gunbc.spark.serving_offer: the serving group's host membership could not be read, so there is nothing to compare against the operator's site allocation and the offer's region rests on nothing"

fn spark_serving_route_uncovered_obligation(uncovered: List<HostIdentity>) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([
    "gunbc.spark.serving_offer: the operator's site allocation (gunbc.spark.first_party_serving spark_fleet_site_allocation, 2026-09-11) does not cover this serving group's host(s) ",
    join(map(uncovered, h => h as String), ", "),
    ", so no assertion places them anywhere and the offer's region cannot be constructed. A unit that has moved needs a NEW allocation row, never an edit to the standing one",
  ], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// WHY A MATCHING CONFIGURATION DOES NOT ESTABLISH THE SERVICE EITHER. An earlier spelling refused only when the readback was unreadable or
// reported a different path, and returned the pair-serving service whenever the reported
// configured_model_path MATCHED. That preserved the exact defect the telemetry repair exists to
// remove, one seam over: gunbc.spark.first_party_serving spark_realization_identity says in its own
// annotation that the comparison establishes CONFIGURATION AGREEMENT and not which launch answered,
// and that a consumer may not read a configuration match as identity. This was that consumer,
// reading it as identity, and naming a specific declared deployment on the strength of it.
//
// SO REFUSING THE UNREADABLE AND DIFFERENT-PATH CASES WAS NEVER THE FIX. Those are the easy cases.
// The motivating case is two deployments that SHARE a configuration, which the comparison cannot
// separate -- so no configuration comparison, however wide, establishes the service. What does is a
// different kind of evidence, an observation of which incarnation answered, and the standing below
// requires it before the configuration is consulted at all.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM: it does not detect a foreign launch. Nothing here can distinguish two
// launches sharing a configuration, and a refusal is not a detection. The claim is only the narrow
// one -- matching configuration does not establish the specific deployment service -- which is why
// the region, resting on evidence of a different kind, survives untouched.
data spark_serving_route_service_identity_obligation: NonEmptyStr = "gunbc.spark.serving_offer: gunbc.spark.first_party_serving spark_first_party_route names gunbc.spark.pair_serving_desired spark_pair_serving_desired as the service, which is a claim about WHICH DEPLOYMENT ANSWERED. Host coverage says only where the machines sit, and the models-route readback establishes at most that the process reports the configuration we declared, which two deployments can share. The service is named only when three separate facts hold together: an observation binds the answering endpoint process to an engine incarnation (gunbc.spark.vllm_serving_launch EndpointIncarnationObservation), that incarnation was launched from the digest of the unit we render (LaunchedFromDeclaredUnit), and the readback agrees with the declared configuration. The second and third are about our declaration and can never stand in for the first. The first is produced in production by gunbc.spark.serving_incarnation_observe, which binds the answering endpoint to an engine launch over the fleet key session; this obligation is the one carried when that producer returned no observation on the bind, and it is concatenated after the specific cause so a reader sees which of the three facts was missing."

// THE CAUSE THE IDENTITY JOIN REPORTS, carried into the refusal so a reader learns WHICH situation
// obtained. The refusal is the same for all four; the diagnosis is not, and a reader who cannot tell
// them apart goes looking for the wrong evidence. The matched-configuration case gets the longest
// sentence because it is the one that reads like success -- everything a consumer might mistake for
// identity is present and the service still cannot be named.
//
// IT CONSUMES THE IDENTITY JOIN AND NOT THE TELEMETRY CAUSE, WHICH IS THE REPAIR THIS REVISION
// MAKES. An earlier spelling called gunbc.spark.first_party_serving
// spark_serving_usage_stats_unread_cause -- the TELEMETRY cause, which is the identity join PLUS two
// host checks that exist for the telemetry question. So a realization whose hosts are covered by the
// allocation but one of whose hosts has no OBSERVED FABRIC LANE rendered, as the reason WHICH
// DEPLOYMENT ANSWERED is unestablished, an obligation about reading more lanes -- while this module's
// own spark_serving_route_service_identity_obligation says the discharging capability is evidence
// identifying the deployment, and lane observation is not it. That is DESIGN 3's meaning fork: one
// name, two materially different contracts. It is also the class this change files at
// gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.diagnostic_asserts_standing_no_check_evaluated, by that row's own
// tell -- name the producer the sentence points a reader at, then check the emitting arm: the
// service arm pointed at telemetry evidence no service check consults.
//
// LATENT RATHER THAN FIRING TODAY, recorded because the distinction decides how it is filed: every
// unit in spark_fleet_site_allocation currently has an observed lane, so the mis-rendered arm is not
// reachable with present data. It becomes reachable the moment a unit is allocated before its lane
// is observed -- which is what a hardware move produces, and this module exists because the operator
// moved hardware. The sentences below are the service's own, so the two questions no longer share a
// renderer.
fn spark_realization_identity_cause(realization: SparkServingRealization) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match spark_realization_identity(realization: realization) {
    RealizationMatchesPairServingConfiguration { configured_model_path: p } =>
      join([
        "the engine reports the model path this launch is declared to serve (", p as String,
        "), which is configuration agreement and not identity -- a second deployment configured the same way reports the same string",
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr
    RealizationReportedPathDiffers { reported: rep, declared: dec } =>
      join([
        "the engine reports the model path ", rep as String,
        ", which is not the path the pair-serving launch is declared to serve (", dec as String,
        "), so this readback does not establish the specific service this route would name. It does NOT establish that the process is a different launch: a process that IS the intended launch, running with drifted configuration, reports exactly this way, and a configuration comparison cannot separate those -- which is the inference this module refuses everywhere else and must refuse in its own diagnosis too",
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr
    RealizationDeclaredPathUnpinned =>
      "the pair-serving snapshot is unpinned, so there is no declared configuration to compare the engine's reported one against" as NonEmptyStr
    RealizationReadbackUnresolved =>
      "the route's /v1/models readback did not resolve, so the process reported nothing that bears on which declared launch answered" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// ── THE SERVICE CAUSE: WHICH OF THE THREE FACTS IS MISSING ─────────────────────────────────────
//
// Each arm is one missing fact, checked in a fixed order: the incarnation observation first, then
// whether that observation is about THIS endpoint and these hosts, then the declared unit's digest and
// the launch's provenance against it, and last the configuration. The observation comes first because
// it is the one fact nothing about our declaration can supply. A reader who sees
// ServiceIncarnationUnobserved therefore knows the other facts were not consulted, which is also why
// that arm carries the configuration diagnosis separately rather than as a verdict.
type SparkServiceIdentityCause
  = ServiceIncarnationUnobserved { configuration: SparkRealizationIdentity, obligation: NonEmptyStr }
  | ServiceObservationNamesAnotherEndpoint { observed: NonEmptyStr, answering: NonEmptyStr }
  | ServiceIncarnationOutsideRealization { incarnation: NonEmptyStr }
  | ServiceDeclaredUnitDigestUnavailable { obligation: NonEmptyStr }
  | ServiceIncarnationNotFromDeclaredUnit { provenance: LaunchUnitProvenance }
  | ServiceConfigurationDisagrees { configuration: SparkRealizationIdentity }

// THE DECLARED SIDE OF THE UNIT COMPARISON IS MINTED AT THE RENDER BOUNDARY
// (gunbc.spark.pair_serving_realization spark_pair_digest_unit_render): SHA-256 of the exact
// rendered systemd unit bytes, or a refusal that carries the render/hash refusal. This module
// consumes that standing; it does not hash. A structural fingerprint remains the wrong family.

data spark_serving_incarnation_unobserved_obligation: NonEmptyStr = "gunbc.spark.serving_offer: the engine incarnation the answering endpoint process belongs to was not observed on this bind. gunbc.spark.serving_incarnation_observe is the producer -- it binds the answering exporter to a gunbc.spark.vllm_serving_launch VllmServingLaunch over the fleet key session -- and this standing is reached when that producer returned no observation for a specific reason it names: no fleet ssh context on this bind, the process not found or ambiguous at the exported start instant, hidepid hiding /proc, or the container image id unread. That last needs gunbc.spark.managed_access_bootstrap InspectServingContainer (installed through gunbc.spark.managed_grant_install): the unprivileged facts -- unit, start-tick identity, cgroup container id -- are established without it, but the image digest that completes the launch is read only through the inspect leg, so a host without that grant fails the inspect command (sudo denies) with the container named"

fn spark_launch_unit_provenance_text(provenance: LaunchUnitProvenance) -> String {
  match provenance {
    LaunchedFromDeclaredUnit { declared: d } => join(["launched from the declared unit ", serialize_content_hash(hash: d) as String], "")
    LaunchedFromDifferentUnit { observed: o, declared: d } =>
      join(["the incarnation's unit digest ", serialize_content_hash(hash: o) as String, " is not the declared head unit's ", serialize_content_hash(hash: d) as String], "")
    LaunchUnitDigestsIncomparable => "the incarnation's unit digest and the declared unit digest are in different hash families, so they cannot be compared"
  }
}

fn spark_service_identity_cause_text(cause: SparkServiceIdentityCause, realization: SparkServingRealization) -> String {
  match cause {
    ServiceIncarnationUnobserved { configuration: _, obligation: o } =>
      join([o as String, ". The readback, which cannot identify the deployment on its own, reports: ", spark_realization_identity_cause(realization: realization) as String], "")
    ServiceObservationNamesAnotherEndpoint { observed: ob, answering: an } =>
      join(["the incarnation observation is about endpoint process ", ob as String, ", not the process that answered this route, ", an as String], "")
    ServiceIncarnationOutsideRealization { incarnation: i } =>
      join(["the observed incarnation ", i as String, " is not on this realization's group and hosts"], "")
    ServiceDeclaredUnitDigestUnavailable { obligation: o } => o as String
    ServiceIncarnationNotFromDeclaredUnit { provenance: p } => spark_launch_unit_provenance_text(provenance: p)
    ServiceConfigurationDisagrees { configuration: _ } =>
      join(["the incarnation is observed and was launched from the declared unit, but the readback disagrees: ", spark_realization_identity_cause(realization: realization) as String], "")
  }
}

fn spark_service_unidentified(realization: SparkServingRealization, cause: SparkServiceIdentityCause) -> SparkServingRoute {
  ServingRouteServiceUnidentified {
    allocation: spark_fleet_site_allocation.allocation,
    cause: cause,
    obligation: join([
      "the operator's dated site allocation covers every host of this realization, so the REGION is established and is not in question. The SERVICE is not. ",
      spark_serving_route_service_identity_obligation as String,
      " For this realization: ",
      spark_service_identity_cause_text(cause: cause, realization: realization),
    ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
  }
}

fn spark_configuration_matches(identity: SparkRealizationIdentity) -> Bool {
  match identity {
    RealizationMatchesPairServingConfiguration { configured_model_path: _ } => true
    RealizationReportedPathDiffers { reported: _, declared: _ } => false
    RealizationDeclaredPathUnpinned => false
    RealizationReadbackUnresolved => false
  }
}

fn spark_service_from_observation(realization: SparkServingRealization, declared_unit: DeclaredUnitDigestStanding, observation: EndpointIncarnationObservation) -> SparkServingRoute {
  let observed_key = vllm_endpoint_process_launch_key(launch: observation.endpoint)
  let answering_key = vllm_endpoint_process_launch_key(launch: realization.endpoint)
  if (observed_key as String) != (answering_key as String) {
    spark_service_unidentified(realization: realization, cause: ServiceObservationNamesAnotherEndpoint { observed: observed_key, answering: answering_key })
  } else if (fabric_group_wire(g: observation.launch.group) as String) != (fabric_group_wire(g: realization.endpoint.group) as String)
      || !any(realization.hosts, h => host_identity_eq(a: h, b: observation.launch.head)) {
    spark_service_unidentified(realization: realization, cause: ServiceIncarnationOutsideRealization { incarnation: launch_wire(i: observation.launch) as NonEmptyStr })
  } else {
    match declared_unit {
      DeclaredUnitDigestUnavailable { obligation: o } =>
        spark_service_unidentified(realization: realization, cause: ServiceDeclaredUnitDigestUnavailable { obligation: o })
      DeclaredUnitDigested { unit_name: _, digest: declared } =>
        match launch_unit_provenance(launch: observation.launch, declared: declared) {
          LaunchedFromDifferentUnit { observed: o, declared: d } =>
            spark_service_unidentified(realization: realization, cause: ServiceIncarnationNotFromDeclaredUnit { provenance: LaunchedFromDifferentUnit { observed: o, declared: d } })
          LaunchUnitDigestsIncomparable =>
            spark_service_unidentified(realization: realization, cause: ServiceIncarnationNotFromDeclaredUnit { provenance: LaunchUnitDigestsIncomparable })
          LaunchedFromDeclaredUnit { declared: _ } => {
            let identity = spark_realization_identity(realization: realization)
            if spark_configuration_matches(identity: identity) {
              ServingRouteOperatorAsserted {
                route: spark_first_party_route(),
                allocation: spark_fleet_site_allocation.allocation,
                answered_by: observation,
              }
            } else {
              spark_service_unidentified(realization: realization, cause: ServiceConfigurationDisagrees { configuration: identity })
            }
          }
        }
    }
  }
}

fn spark_serving_route_standing(realization: SparkServingRealization, declared_unit: DeclaredUnitDigestStanding) -> SparkServingRoute {
  let hosts = realization.hosts
  if count(hosts) == 0 {
    ServingRouteRegionUnobserved { obligation: spark_serving_route_hosts_unreadable_obligation }
  } else {
    let uncovered = spark_site_uncovered_units(hosts: hosts)
    if count(uncovered) > 0 {
      ServingRouteRegionUnobserved { obligation: spark_serving_route_uncovered_obligation(uncovered: uncovered) }
    } else {
      match realization.incarnation {
        EndpointIncarnationUnobserved { obligation: o } =>
          spark_service_unidentified(realization: realization, cause: ServiceIncarnationUnobserved {
            configuration: spark_realization_identity(realization: realization),
            obligation: o,
          })
        EndpointIncarnationObserved { observation: obs } =>
          spark_service_from_observation(realization: realization, declared_unit: declared_unit, observation: obs)
      }
    }
  }
}

type SparkOfferBinding<P>
  = SparkOfferBound { bound: SparkOfferProjection<P> }
  | SparkOfferBindingRefused { group_wire: NonEmptyStr, cause: NonEmptyStr }

// THE SHAPE IS DELIBERATELY UNSTATED RATHER THAN INVENTED. Every axis Shape offers would be a number
// nobody measured for this group, and envelope.dag rules that an absent axis is a requirement nobody
// expressed, not a requirement satisfied by everything.
//
// WHY NO SIDECAR LISTS WHAT IS MISSING. An earlier spelling carried a SparkServingUnexpressed list on
// the projection, naming the KV budget, the tensor-parallel unit, interactive quality and isolation
// as axes Shape cannot hold. Nothing read it. Review 62334 is right on both counts: its rank count
// re-minted a fact gunbc.spark.pair_serving_desired already decides as length(hosts), and an unread
// list of nullary variants is prose in a type's costume -- DESIGN 3c's dangling declaration, which is
// red however well modelled. The two departures that carry a machine consequence are declared drops
// that a projection reads (gunbc.rung_drop serving_cost_axes_asserted_unmeasured and
// serving_offer_identity_is_the_exporter_process); the rest is the irreducible rationale above, which
// is what an annotation is for. Interactive quality in particular is not a missing Shape axis at all:
// this cut enforces it as ADMISSION, which is the whole point of the co-tenancy wall.
fn spark_serving_shape() -> Shape {
  Shape { hard: HardRequirements { threads: hardware_thread_count(count: 0) }, envelope: unstated_envelope() }
}

fn bind_spark_serving_offer<P>(
  principal: P,
  executor: P,
  launch: VllmEndpointProcessLaunch,
  served_model: NonEmptyStr,
  trust_domain: TrustDomainRef,
  isolation: IsolationProfile,
  seats: Int,
  availability: ServingFrontDoorAvailabilityVerdict,
  route: SparkServingRoute,
) -> SparkOfferBinding<P> {
  match route {
    ServingRouteRegionUnobserved { obligation: why } =>
      SparkOfferBindingRefused { group_wire: fabric_group_wire(g: launch.group), cause: why }
    ServingRouteServiceUnidentified { allocation: _, cause: _, obligation: why } =>
      SparkOfferBindingRefused { group_wire: fabric_group_wire(g: launch.group), cause: why }
    ServingRouteOperatorAsserted { route: asserted, allocation: _, answered_by: _ } =>
      bind_spark_serving_offer_on_route(
        principal: principal, executor: executor, launch: launch, served_model: served_model,
        trust_domain: trust_domain, isolation: isolation, seats: seats,
        availability: availability, route: asserted,
      )
    ServingRouteObserved { route: r0 } =>
      bind_spark_serving_offer_on_route(
        principal: principal, executor: executor, launch: launch, served_model: served_model,
        trust_domain: trust_domain, isolation: isolation, seats: seats,
        availability: availability, route: r0,
      )
  }
}

// THE TWO ROUTE-BEARING ARMS BIND THROUGH ONE BODY, so an offer built from an asserted site and one
// built from an observed site cannot drift apart. What distinguishes them is entirely upstream --
// which arm a producer could honestly construct -- and nothing downstream of the route treats them
// differently, because a ProviderRoute is a ProviderRoute once it exists. Duplicating the binder
// per arm would have been the fork that lets exactly that stop being true.
fn bind_spark_serving_offer_on_route<P>(
  principal: P,
  executor: P,
  launch: VllmEndpointProcessLaunch,
  served_model: NonEmptyStr,
  trust_domain: TrustDomainRef,
  isolation: IsolationProfile,
  seats: Int,
  availability: ServingFrontDoorAvailabilityVerdict,
  route: ProviderRoute,
) -> SparkOfferBinding<P> {
  match spark_serving_readiness_of(verdict: availability, launch: launch) {
      ServingUnobserved { obligation: why } =>
        SparkOfferBindingRefused { group_wire: fabric_group_wire(g: launch.group), cause: why }
      ServingObserved { ready_at: at, receipt: r } =>
        SparkOfferBound {
          bound: SparkOfferProjection {
            offer: SupplierOffer {
              id: FabricIdentity { principal: principal, key: spark_serving_offer_key(launch: launch) },
              route: route,
              executor: executor,
              shape: spark_serving_shape(),
              capabilities: spark_serving_capability(),
              trust_domain: trust_domain,
              isolation: isolation,
              quantity_bound: seats,
              ready_at: at,
              quote: spark_serving_quote(),
              billing_quantum: spark_serving_billing_quantum,
              evidence: ObservedSupply { observed_at: at, receipt: r },
            },
            launch: launch,
            served_model: served_model,
          },
        }
  }
}
